                         Patch Metadata - Gen 1/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                          
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reduce_params_grouped_sharing_with_ising_entangler                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 24 distinct trainable parameters (one RY + two RZ per qubit,      │
│                          │ i.e., 3 per-qubit parameters × 8 qubits), which the scoring function penalizes heavily    │
│                          │ since parameter economy is rewarded at every count. To cut parameter count while          │
│                          │ preserving expressivity for separating the hardest validation groups, I group qubits into │
│                          │ pairs (0-1, 2-3, 4-5, 6-7) and share single-qubit rotation parameters within each pair.   │
│                          │ This reduces the RY layer from 8 params to 4, and each RZ layer from 8 params to 4, for   │
│                          │ 12 total single-qubit params instead of 24. I also replace the fixed CZ entangling ring   │
│                          │ with parametrized ZZ (Ising) gates sharing a single parameter across the whole ring,      │
│                          │ which adds tunable entanglement strength (helpful for correlating qubit pairs relevant to │
│                          │ hidden difficulty groups) while adding only 1 new parameter. Net distinct parameter       │
│                          │ count: 4 (RY groups) + 4 (RZ pre groups) + 4 (RZ post groups) + 1 (shared ZZ) = 13,       │
│                          │ roughly half of the original 24, which should substantially boost parameter_efficiency    │
│                          │ and reduce train/val gap risk, while keeping enough per-pair granularity to maintain the  │
│                          │ strong margin structure already observed (worst_group_margin 0.35).                       │
