│ patch_description        │ The scoring rule states that fewer distinct trainable parameters always yields a higher   │
│                          │ score, with no diminishing returns, and the empirical trend across the three prior        │
│                          │ generations (24→8→5 distinct params, score 0.60→0.65→0.69) confirms this strongly         │
│                          │ outweighs other metric differences. The current ansatz still uses two separate RY groups  │
│                          │ (ry_a, ry_b) and two separate RZ groups (rz_a, rz_b) that are symmetric across the        │
│                          │ 8-qubit register. Since accuracy, margins, and convergence are already saturated          │
│                          │ (train/val/test accuracy = 1.00, margins ~0.35-0.73) regardless of this split, collapsing │
│                          │ each symmetric pair into a single shared parameter (ry_a=ry_b -> "ry_shared", rz_a=rz_b   │
│                          │ -> "rz_shared") reduces distinct parameter count from 5 to 3 without changing circuit     │
│                          │ depth, gate count, or entangling structure. This should further improve parameter         │
│                          │ efficiency and the combined score while preserving the expressivity needed for perfect    │
│                          │ train/val/test accuracy, since the previous merge (rz pre/post sharing) already showed no │
│                          │ accuracy loss.                                                                            │
