│ patch_description        │ The evidence across trials shows that fewer distinct trainable parameters consistently    │
│                          │ score higher, with the 4-distinct-parameter seed (ry_a, ry_b, rz, zz_ring) clearly        │
│                          │ outperforming the current 6-parameter variant (0.70 vs 0.67) despite very similar         │
│                          │ accuracy/margin numbers. Since parameter economy dominates the score with no diminishing  │
│                          │ returns, we push further by merging the two RY groups (ry_a, ry_b) into a single shared   │
│                          │ "ry" parameter, and simplifying back to the RZ-ZZring-RZ sandwich structure (drop the     │
│                          │ extra XX ring and rz_a/rz_b split) that already proved effective. This yields only 3      │
│                          │ distinct parameters (ry, rz, zz_ring) while keeping the same qualitative circuit motif: a │
│                          │ global RY layer, a phase layer around a fully-shared ZZ entangling ring. Reducing gate    │
│                          │ count and depth also helps convergence speed and train/test gap. The intended effect is   │
│                          │ to trade a small amount of raw expressiveness for a substantially better                  │
│                          │ parameter-efficiency term and faster/more stable convergence, which the scoring function  │
│                          │ rewards heavily.                                                                          │
