│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ dual_diagonal_sandwich_block                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses one entangling block with cross-couplings along one diagonal set  │
│                          │ ([0,4],[1,5],[2,6],[3,7]). To increase robustness for the hardest validation group        │
│                          │ without adding any new trainable parameters, we duplicate the entire                      │
│                          │ RY→CNOT→RZ→ZZ(ring+diagonal)→CNOT→RZ block a second time, reusing the exact same four     │
│                          │ parameter names (ry_a, ry_b, rz_p, zz_ring), but switch the long-range diagonal couplings │
│                          │ in the second block to the complementary set ([0,5],[1,6],[2,7],[3,4]). This gives the    │
│                          │ circuit access to both diagonal connectivity patterns of the qubit register while keeping │
│                          │ n_distinct_params=4 (same parameter efficiency score), at the cost of increased           │
│                          │ depth/gate count. Covering both diagonal directions should improve the worst-group margin │
│                          │ (the dominant score term) since the hardest samples may rely on correlations that only    │
│                          │ one diagonal set captures. This directly follows the given recommendation and mirrors the │
│                          │ sandwich motif that already improved margin/generalization in prior iterations (seed2     │
│                          │ outperformed seed1 by adding CNOT ladders and diagonal ZZ couplings at zero parameter     │
│                          │ cost).                                                                                    │
