                         Patch Metadata - Gen 37/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two top-scoring (0.71) seed variants, taken a step further along the     │
│                          │ "fewer distinct parameters always wins" guidance that the task description states         │
│                          │ explicitly. Both 0.71 seeds already reduced the trainable-parameter set to {ry_a, ry_b,   │
│                          │ rz_p, zz_ring} (4 distinct names) by sharing an RY parameter across each half of the      │
│                          │ register, a single global RZ phase before/after the entangling ring, and a single shared  │
│                          │ ZZ-ring coupling (including long-range "hypercube" cross-couplings) that costs nothing    │
│                          │ extra in parameter count. The current mutant that added a second rz_a/rz_b split (5       │
│                          │ distinct params, more gates, no CNOT mixing) scored worse (0.69), confirming that adding  │
│                          │ parameters or dropping the zero-cost CNOT mixing layers hurts the metric even when        │
│                          │ accuracy stays perfect.                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This crossover keeps every zero-cost structural idea from the winning seeds               │
│                          │ (forward+backward CNOT ladders for mixing, global RZ phase reused twice, ZZ ring with     │
│                          │ long-range "opposite-qubit" couplings all sharing one coupling parameter) but merges the  │
│                          │ two RY groups (`ry_a`, `ry_b`) into a single shared `ry_a`, cutting the                   │
│                          │ distinct-parameter count from 4 down to 3 (ry_a, rz_p, zz_ring). To compensate for the    │
│                          │ reduced expressivity from merging the RY groups and to help the worst-group margin (the   │
│                          │ dominant score term), an additional shared-parameter "skip-2" ZZ shell                    │
│                          │ (0-2,1-3,2-4,3-5,4-6,5-7,6-0,7-1) is added — still reusing the single `zz_ring`           │
│                          │ parameter, so no new trainable names are introduced, but it enriches connectivity beyond  │
│                          │ the nearest-neighbor + opposite-qubit couplings of the seeds, aiming to better separate   │
│                          │ hard validation groups while remaining as parameter-economical as possible.               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This design targets: fewer distinct parameters (3 vs 4) for a higher parameter-economy    │
│                          │ score, at least as much or richer entangling structure to preserve/raise                  │
│                          │ worst_group_margin, and no duplicate motif repetition (which was shown to hurt            │
│                          │ convergence/param count in the current program).                                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
