│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Building on the previous 4-parameter ring/ladder design (score 0.71, worst_group_margin   │
│                          │ 0.36), this variant follows the recommendation to split the single shared "rz_p" phase    │
│                          │ parameter into two distinct trainable names: "rz_pre" (applied before the parametrized ZZ │
│                          │ entangling ring) and "rz_post" (applied after it, following the backward CNOT ladder).    │
│                          │ Reusing one shared RZ parameter for both pre- and post-entangling phases collapses two    │
│                          │ structurally different roles (setting up the entangling interference pattern vs. reading  │
│                          │ it back out) into one degree of freedom, which likely limited the achievable worst-group  │
│                          │ margin. Splitting them costs only one extra trainable parameter (5 distinct instead of 4) │
│                          │ but restores the phase flexibility needed to shape the entangling interference pattern    │
│                          │ independently on each side, which prior experiments identified as important for margin    │
│                          │ quality. All other zero-cost structure (RY group split, CNOT ladders, fully shared        │
│                          │ long-range ZZ ring) is kept unchanged, preserving the parameter economy and convergence   │
│                          │ speed already achieved.                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Argumentation: since parameter economy matters at every count but robust worst-group      │
│                          │ separation dominates the score, trading one extra parameter for meaningfully more         │
│                          │ expressive phase control is worth it if it improves worst_group_margin, which prior notes │
│                          │ flagged as the key lever. The other structural elements (fixed CNOT ladders for           │
│                          │ connectivity, single shared "zz_ring" for the entangling interactions, RY group           │
│                          │ asymmetry) remain as in the best-performing prior variant to avoid disturbing what        │
│                          │ already works.                                                                            │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0464                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5                                                      │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
