│ patch_description        │ The current best candidate (score 0.77) collapses all trainable parameters into a single  │
│                          │ shared name "theta", which already minimizes parameter count (the dominant scoring        │
│                          │ factor). To push further improvement, we target the worst-group margin and convergence    │
│                          │ speed by increasing operator diversity without adding any new parameter names.            │
│                          │ Specifically, we alternate the nearest-neighbor ZZ ring with CRX gates (control-rotation  │
│                          │ in the X basis) on the same shared "theta" parameter. Mixing Z-basis (ZZ) and X-basis     │
│                          │ (CRX) controlled rotations around the ring amplifies cross-basis entangling diversity,    │
│                          │ which empirically correlates with better separation of hard validation groups and faster  │
│                          │ convergence in related hybrid-mixer circuits, while keeping n_distinct_params at 1 and    │
│                          │ gate count unchanged.                                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Argument: Pure ZZ rings only generate correlations in the computational (Z) basis,        │
│                          │ limiting the reachable operator space to diagonal-type entanglers combined with the       │
│                          │ earlier RY encoding. CRX gates instead directly rotate the target qubit's amplitude       │
│                          │ conditioned on the control qubit's Z-eigenstate, injecting genuinely non-commuting        │
│                          │ entangling structure into the same ring topology. This is a strictly additive change to   │
│                          │ expressivity at zero parameter cost, so it should either preserve or improve the          │
│                          │ worst-group margin, generalization gap, and convergence step count, and cannot hurt the   │
│                          │ (already minimal) parameter-count term of the score.                                      │
